## 2. Key technical and architectural decisions

### 2.1 Reverse-engineered PIB mechanics (all verified against the live site)

- **Advanced Search is an ASP.NET client callback, not a form post.** The search
  box has no `name` attribute; the button calls `getSearchPara(1)`. POST to
  `AdvSearch.aspx` with `__CALLBACKID=__Page` and `__CALLBACKPARAM` =
  pipe-joined string:
  `1|{text}|{fDay}|{fMon}|{fYear}|{tDay}|{tMon}|{tYear}|{ministry}|{searchtype}|{nav}`.
  `__VIEWSTATE` is required (without it you get the full 111KB page back), but
  one prime per *session* suffices. The visible `<select name=...>` attributes
  are decoys and are never posted.
- **HARD 1000-RESULT CAP, silent.** Max 50 pages x 20. Page 51 returns empty and
  the `Next` link vanishes — no warning, no total-count field. A 2003–2026
  search for `"artificial intelligence"` returned 1000 hits, **all from
  2025–2026**; every hit from 2010, 2013, 2016 was silently dropped. Results are
  newest-first, so truncation always eats the oldest material.
- **Pagination is a session cursor, not a page index.** Last payload field:
  `1` = new search, `3` = next, `2` = previous. No random access. A fresh
  session calling `3` first returns 0 rows. State lives in the session cookie;
  separate cookie jars are isolated.
- **Search is whole-word phrase matching**, order-sensitive
  (`"intelligence artificial"` = 0 hits vs 154 for `"artificial intelligence"`),
  no substring matching (`artific` = 0). Case-insensitive. A `|` in the query
  silently returns 0 — sanitize it, it is the payload delimiter.
- **Individual releases have a stateless direct URL:**
  `archive2/PrintRelease.aspx?relid=N`. No cookies, viewstate, or session.
  Structure: `<title>` = title, `<div id="thd1">` = ministry + `DD-Month-YYYY
  HH:MM IST`, `<div id="condiv">` = centred heading then body.
  Three other URL patterns (`erelease.aspx`, `newsite/erelease.aspx`,
  `pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=`) return **HTTP 200 but ignore the
  id** and serve unrelated pages. Do not trust a 200 here.
- **Missing records return 302** -> `mainpage.aspx` -> 404. Recorded as `absent`.
- Advanced Search **day = "All" (value 0) silently returns zero results**.
- Advanced Search date filters are fuzzy by ~1 day; dedupe by relid.

### 2.2 Enumeration vs. search-driven crawling — resolved, with caveats

Decision: **enumerate the full relid space**, then run keyword expansion locally.

The user correctly challenged this mid-session: the 1000-cap alone does **not**
justify enumeration, since adaptive date-slicing solves it. That criticism was
accepted. Honest cost comparison:

| Approach | Requests | Wall clock |
|---|---|---|
| Search-driven, sliced by year | ~45,000 | ~2 h |
| Full enumeration | 292,729 | ~12 h |

The two arguments that genuinely hold up (everything else is convenience):

1. **Recall is unmeasurable from a keyword-selected corpus.** There is no
   denominator. The 87–98% recall figure below exists only because complete
   2025 data was available to check against.
2. **Discovery patterns need raw text.** Searching `GPT` returns 2 releases,
   both meaning "General Purpose Technologies"; the 14 real ones (ChatGPT,
   **MausamGPT**, **BharatGPT**) are invisible to whole-word search. The
   proximity rule that found ASTR / CyTrain / SabhaSaar / Digital Twin cannot be
   expressed as any search query.

Everything else (unlimited free expansion rounds, re-analysis, robustness) is
real but secondary. **For a one-shot question with a good seed list,
search-slicing would have worked.**

### 2.3 Rate limiting — measured, and an earlier conclusion retracted

- No `robots.txt` on either `archive.pib.gov.in` or `pib.gov.in` (both serve the
  app's generic error page with a misleading 200). No published crawl-delay.
- Server latency: **~50 ms** for `PrintRelease`, ~200 ms for the search callback.
- A bounded escalation probe reached **13.8 req/s** sustained with zero errors —
  no ceiling found; the limit hit was the serial test harness.
- **Retracted claim:** a 15-second latency spike at 19:18 was initially blamed on
  running at 10 req/s. It was **not** — the same target rate ran clean
  afterwards. It was a transient, amplified into a two-hour collapse by a
  governor bug (see 2.4).
- **Also retracted:** an earlier claim that the crawl was CPU-bound at 5.7 req/s.
  Profiling showed decode+parse = **1.5 ms**, DB insert ~0 ms. Never CPU-bound.

### 2.4 Crawler governor design (three bugs found and fixed)

1. **Backoff cascade (root cause of the 2-hour slowdown).** After a backoff the
   samples that triggered it stayed in the latency window, so the next check read
   the same spike and backed off again — four halvings in 46 seconds down to
   1 req/s. **Fix:** clear the latency window on backoff.
2. **Twitchy trigger.** Backoff fired at 3x baseline; with a ~30 ms baseline that
   is 90 ms, reachable by ordinary jitter. **Fix:** require both 3x relative
   *and* a 400 ms absolute floor.
3. **No ceiling memory.** The governor only ramped up, so it would oscillate into
   a bad rate forever. **Fix:** a rate that degrades the server is remembered and
   never retried; ceiling only learned when tripping *near* it (a stall at a low
   rate is a transient, not capacity); ceiling creeps back 15% after a long clean
   stretch so one blip cannot ratchet to the floor permanently.

Validated against simulated servers with limits at 6, 8, and unlimited, plus a
run with four multi-second stalls: no overshoot, no collapse.

### 2.5 Process durability

The crawl **died once** because it ran as a child of the Claude Code session with
output piped through `tee` — when the agent process exited, `tee` died and the
crawler took a SIGPIPE. Now launched with `nohup`, direct redirect (no pipeline),
and **verified reparented to PPID 1**. It survives the session closing.

> Consequence: the assistant is **not** notified when it completes. Check with
> `python3 status.py`.

### 2.6 Keyword expansion design (liberal, per user instruction)

The user explicitly directed: **do not gate candidates on unusual frequency.**
Anything that looks AI-adjacent (a `smart-` prefix, a `-GPT` suffix) goes in.
Precision is recovered later at the classification stage; recall lost at
candidate generation is gone permanently. Frequency lift **orders the review
queue, never excludes**.

Two channels, treated differently:

- **Pattern channel** — morphological shapes, shown regardless of statistics.
- **Phrase channel** — ordinary n-grams, gated on lift >= 3.0, because ranking
  purely by novelty surfaces the most *generic* words ("narendra modi", "sabha",
  "given" — all lift ~1.0, i.e. no association with AI at all).

**Patterns dropped after measurement:** `e-/i-*`, `*Setu/Mitra`,
`*Vision/Netra` produced **74 of 76 candidates and nearly all noise** —
e-rickshaws, `e-Mohammad` (from *Jaish-e-Mohammad*), `Ram Setu` (a mythological
land bridge), "long-term vision". These match Indian government *digital-
programme naming conventions*, which track digitisation, not AI.

**Patterns kept:** `*GPT`, `Smart*`, `AI-*`, `*Drishti`, `*bot`, `camelCase`.

**Added:** a **proximity channel** — a capitalised name near an accepted AI term
*and* followed by a system descriptor (platform/portal/system/app/tool/model/
engine/...). The descriptor is what separates `Divya Drishti tool` from
`Shri Jitendra Singh` — person names are never followed by "platform".

